<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Patagonia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Patagonia is the southern end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, shared by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and Chile, stretching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a> river and the southern Andes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tierra-del-fuego">Tierra del Fuego</a>. It combines mountains, steppe plateaus and a fjord coast, and was long the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehuelche">Tehuelche</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mapuche">Mapuche</a> and other peoples. Argentina and Chile occupied it in the late 19th century, Argentina through the military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conquest-of-the-desert">Conquest of the Desert</a> of 1878 to 1885.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/patagonia.jpg" alt="Patagonia" width="320" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pat_map.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
